    1  A   ablation_onset           0.204    93.76   1.794e+08  stylized
          Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on
          (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's
          light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~94 km as low-confidence.
          Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin,
          volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
    2  -   first_light              0.336    89.66   1.370e+09  info
          L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value
          depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
    3  Y   first_yield              2.355    27.00   1.748e+13  physical
          Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 30 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and
          heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5
          / H_f in [R22]).
    4  F   flare                    2.409    25.34   5.725e+13  stylized
          Light rises x11.6 in 0.16 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated
          with: yield gen 0 at 26.9 km (95% of m0); split gen 0 at 24.1 km (90% of m0); yield gen 1 at
          24.1 km (34% of m0) +4 more. Dust groups supply 18% of the rise and 17% of the light at the
          end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing
          here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
    5  B   first_breakup            2.445    24.23   1.112e+14  stylized
          Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as
          dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of
          a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification.
          Light grew x6.4 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
    6  P   peak                     2.448    24.14   1.342e+15  physical
          Maximum light. It sits ~+3.4 km from the peak of energy deposition (20.8 km) because the
          luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 31.7 km/s).
    7  D   rolloff                  2.461    23.75   4.204e+14  physical
          Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.01 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used
          up (speed 98% of v0, 76% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue
          of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
    8  F   flare                    2.530    21.67   2.600e+14  stylized
